(b) The holding of meetings of the Panel and the bases on which decisions of the Panel are made shall be set out in the regulation made under this Act.

Responsibilities and powers of the Panel
22. (a) The Panel established under Article 17 of this Act shall carry out and complete the following matters, in the manner set out in this Act.

(1) examining whether a category exists under Article 12 of this Act in which the enforcement of the death penalty is temporarily prohibited;

(2) examining whether the procedures for enforcing the death penalty determined under this Act are being completed in the manner determined in accordance with this Act;

(3) giving the Maldives Correctional Service authorisation to enforce the death penalty under Article 37 of this Act.

(b) In carrying out the responsibilities set out in subsection (a) of this Article, the Panel shall have the following powers.

(1) obtaining true copies of the originals, or the originals, of documents, letters and reports held at the relevant State offices;

(2) instructing State offices to send to the Panel the originals of the relevant documents, letters and reports held at those offices and submitted to the Panel, or

[stamp] [signature]

any copy thereof;

(3) making visits to clarify information, including visiting the prison in which the convicted person is being held;

(4) clarifying information by questioning, or by visiting, any member of the family of the convicted person;

(5) appointing technical professionals of the relevant health field to ascertain the physical and mental soundness of the convicted person.

Determination of the date and time for enforcing the death penalty
23. (a) Within a maximum of 30 (thirty) days from the date on which the Panel set out in Article 17 of this Act is established, the Panel shall determine, in accordance with this Article, the date and time at which the death penalty shall be enforced upon the convicted person.

(b) When the Panel determines the date and time under this Article, the following parties shall be informed of that matter in writing within a maximum of 72 (seventy-two) hours of the making of that decision.

(1) the convicted person;

(2) the family of the convicted person;

(3) the legal counsel of the convicted person;

