Chapter Five
Rights of the Convicted Person

Rights to be afforded to the convicted person
26. The fundamental rights shall be fully afforded to the convicted person upon whom the death penalty has been determined, in the manner set out in this Chapter.

(a) submitting a request for pardon of that penalty in accordance with law;

(b) submitting a request for commutation of that penalty in accordance with law;

(c) submitting a request to the Supreme Court for review of the decisions of the court in the manner set out in this Act;

(d) obtaining the assistance of a legal counsel;

(e) family meeting;

(f) making a final will.

For pardon of the sentence or commutation of the penalty
27. (a) Submissions set out in Article 26(a) and (b) of this Act, for pardon of a sentence determining the death penalty or

(1) arranging the means of enforcing the penalty in conformity with Article 33 of this Act, to the standard set out in that Article;

(2) arranging the place where the penalty is to be enforced [unclear], so that the penalty can be enforced in the presence of the parties set out in Article 35 of this Act;

(3) having in place arrangements for treatment that can be given to the convicted person in the event of anything happening to that person, or of anything occurring to that person in an abnormal health condition, during the enforcement of the penalty.

(c) The arrangements for enforcing the death penalty in the manner set out in this Article shall be made and completed at least 10 (ten) days before the date determined for enforcing the penalty under Article 23 of this Act.

Parties required to be present, and parties with the right to be present, when the death penalty is enforced
35. (a) The death penalty shall be enforced upon a person under this Act in the presence of the following parties.

(1) the Commissioner of Prisons or an officer of the Maldives Correctional Service determined by him;

(2) the Commissioner of Police or a police officer determined by him;

(3) the Prosecutor General or a prosecutor determined by him;

(c) When the convicted person dies upon being hanged, that person's death shall be confirmed, and that fact shall be communicated to the parties present at the place where the penalty is enforced.

Handing over the body of the deceased to the family
40. (a) After the death penalty is enforced under Article 39 of this Act, the body of the deceased shall be handed over to the family.

(b) Where the members of the family of the deceased do not wish to take custody of the body, or where there is no family member to take custody of it, arrangements for shrouding and burial shall be made by the Maldives Correctional Service.

Matters prohibited in enforcing the penalty
